data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_RESULT","toolCallId":"call_f1f8d4fddda5403186f04b12","messageId":"08007e1c-fb63-420e-89b0-64b0cc42d16e","content":"{\"ok\":true,\"result\":{\"path\":\"/skills/ui-plugin-development/SKILL.md\",\"content\":\"1: ---\\n2: name: ui-plugin-development\\n3: description: Edit project-owned UI Plugin code directly. Ask only before creating a new Plugin identity or first taking over an npm implementation.\\n4: compatibility: Agent UI Plugin Creator Phase 8 permits writes under project plugins and AppUIModel composition.\\n5: ---\\n6: \\n7: # UI Plugin development\\n8: \\n9: This Skill applies to frontend Plugin source/application authoring. The system\\n10: prompt owns general evidence, authorization and completion rules; this Skill\\n11: adds Plugin-specific contracts, not an additional investigation checklist.\\n12: Use the smallest relevant section/reference. A resolved existing target does not\\n13: require a full capability inventory, a new UI-system decision or a nearest\\n14: consumer example before it can be modified.\\n15: \\n16: ## Ownership and admission\\n17: \\n18: Project-owned application props, children, renderers and scoped styles are ordinary\\n19: editable code. Inspect the exact target and any concrete unknown contract; edit\\n20: within the user's request. Public npm component use does not require implementation\\n21: source, extraction or takeover. Existing source-owned Plugin edits keep the current\\n22: ownership, upstream provenance and BASE; do not repeat successful takeover consent.\\n23: Only actual npm implementation edits require `takeover_plugin_source` and its\\n24: explicit user agreement. Canonical Runtime/Context, vendor and generated registries\\n25: remain protected. A thin wrapper's omitted public seam is not justification for\\n26: requiring takeover.\\n27: \\n28: Ask `prepare_ui_plugin_development` once before creating a NEW Plugin identity,\\n29: including wrapping a component as a new Plugin. After confirmation, implement\\n30: its source, supporting modules, existing parent changes, composition and tests.\\n31: The question result is not a per-file allowlist; changed implementation details\\n32: never require the same development question again. Existing Plugin behavior,\\n33: new interactions and project Service files are ordinary edits. Only actual npm\\n34: implementation changes need the separate source-takeover question.\\n35: For new Plugin manifest/placement and delivery details consult only relevant\\n36: sections of [authoring contracts](references/authoring-contracts.md).\\n37: \\n38: ## Local change evidence\\n39: \\n40: Read the existing files that will change, using the actual supplied sourceRoot and\\n41: owner paths. `manifest.json` declares identity/data/capabilities; `definition.ts`\\n42: joins manifest and component; `index.tsx` owns composition/behavior; imported\\n43: stylesheets own presentation. Read a neighboring consumer only for wiring that\\n44: is actually unknown. An npm implementation need not appear in local search.\\n45: \\n46: `inspect_ui_plugin` returns application ownership, public APIs and parent semantic\\n47: composition where declared. sharedModule selects the installed public module;\\n48: use the root module for presentation controls and /contracts for Plugin types.\\n49: Use returned parameterContracts; follow exact parameterQueries only when a required\\n50: signature is missing. `$element` accesses an array/index element, not React props.\\n51: An unavailable guessed symbol is not a missing capability. Preserve declared\\n52: composition slots when appending controls; replacement props can replace them.\\n53: \\n54: Public types establish invocation shape, not rendered styling. Package-owned\\n55: presentationContract describes raw versus styled components, asset requirements\\n56: and Portal ownership. For a visual defect follow the current owned entry's CSS\\n57: imports and inspect the relevant cascade. Re-querying types cannot establish\\n58: centering. Public overlay composites may own their own Portal and Backdrop;\\n59: respect that composition and retain the existing AgentUIRoot theme container.\\n60: Use the public Trigger/Close/focus APIs. Local dialog state or scoped popup\\n61: positioning does not introduce a Service, Agent action, Runtime replacement or\\n62: source takeover. Verify requested placement, closing and focus in the actual Host.\\n63: \\n64: ## Editing constraints\\n65: \\n66: Freshly read existing edit targets in the current run; old messages/handoff are\\n67: navigation, not current read evidence. `edit_file_from_read` edits a stable range;\\n68: `edit_file` handles small local changes; `mutate_ui_plugin_source` makes one atomic\\n69: Plugin-local transaction with edit/create entries. Re-read and reconcile affected\\n70: files after stale-content errors. Do not hand-edit `registry.generated.ts`, the\\n71: AppUIModel JSON or composition-revision metadata; use their Host tools.\\n72: \\n73: Preserve the target's UI library, enabled capabilities, scope bridge, semantic\\n74: slots and original approval/interrupt/error renderers. New user/screen-reader copy\\n75: uses the unified domain locale namespace with en-US/zh-CN parity; never patch\\n76: vendor for localization. Read locale targets when changing them, not for every\\n77: style edit. Do not invent new per-Plugin i18n or Service abstractions.\\n78: \\n79: Styles must be imported and scoped to owned classes/data attributes; no global\\n80: resets, Host ancestor selectors, Runtime/Portal changes or vendor overrides.\\n81: Own box-sizing, min-width and responsive maximum size. Flex/grid children must\\n82: shrink and long text wrap; overlays need bounded dimensions appropriate to the\\n83: actual container. Static heuristics are not visual acceptance. The specialized\\n84: responsive-repair proposal tool is not currently exposed by any Creator tool\\n85: group. For ordinary authorized responsive CSS changes, freshly read the owned\\n86: stylesheet, use the available file-edit tools and validate the current revision.\\n87: If the user explicitly requests the unavailable specialized proposal tool,\\n88: explain that limitation; do not claim to have invoked it.\\n89: \\n90: ## Validation and outcome\\n91: \\n92: Validate source changes at the current revision; include the build for new visual\\n93: Plugins or dependency/style integration as required. Composition changes use fresh\\n94: AppUIModel evidence and normal Host mutation/verification tools. Creation/catalog\\n95: synchronization is not mounting: newly created capabilities must be composed when\\n96: requested, and the final revision validated. Consume checks already performed by\\n97: the Host rather than duplicating them. Respect returned repair limits.\\n98: \\n99: Keep static, Runtime and browser evidence distinct. Current tests/types or empty\\n100: Runtime errors do not prove popup geometry, keyboard behavior or focus. Validate\\n101: the requested interactions using available authorized browser/test evidence; report\\n102: missing infrastructure or stale evidence precisely. Do not label incomplete work\\n103: as already satisfied or reopen unrelated discovery when verification is unavailable.\\n104: \",\"hash\":\"d16638fb9d258001f57838e0d1ed8825997e8f323dc570b4ef1990f589272da5\",\"totalLines\":104,\"nextOffset\":null}}","role":"tool"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_START","toolCallId":"call_473a5a0c2b9c4e6eac49252c","toolCallName":"read_file"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_ARGS","toolCallId":"call_473a5a0c2b9c4e6eac49252c","delta":"{\"file_path\":\"/skills/ui-plugin-development/references/authoring-contracts.md\",\"limit\":400,\"offset\":0}"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_END","toolCallId":"call_473a5a0c2b9c4e6eac49252c"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_RESULT","toolCallId":"call_473a5a0c2b9c4e6eac49252c","messageId":"4088c5a7-d721-4699-a811-90e7345648e6","content":"{\"ok\":true,\"result\":{\"path\":\"/skills/ui-plugin-development/references/authoring-contracts.md\",\"content\":\"1: # Task-specific Plugin authoring contracts\\n2: \\n3: Consult only sections relevant to the actual change. Creating a new Plugin,\\n4: changing a Service or binding Agent data has obligations beyond an existing\\n5: owned presentation edit. This reference grants no authority.\\n6: \\n7: ## Creator Authoring Contract\\n8: \\n9: Runtime-compatible, Composition-compatible, and Creator-operable are separate\\n10: decisions:\\n11: \\n12: - Runtime-compatible means the Plugin can be activated by the UI Runtime.\\n13: - Composition-compatible means its Layout or child Slot contract is valid.\\n14: - Creator-operable means the Host can discover, add, and restore it\\n15:   deterministically.\\n16: \\n17: The existing `manifest.authoring` fields are the Creator Authoring Contract.\\n18: Do not add `creatorReady`, a readiness score, or Plugin-specific Creator logic.\\n19: The Host derives readiness from the manifest, capabilities, and child Slot\\n20: contracts; it does not inspect React source to guess placement.\\n21: \\n22: Before writing source, classify the Plugin and decide whether users should be\\n23: able to ask Creator to add or restore it:\\n24: \\n25: - A visual Plugin intended for natural-language Add/Restore declares semantic\\n26:   `authoring.intents` and a deterministic `defaultPlacement`.\\n27: - A relative `before`/`after` `defaultPlacement` requires\\n28:   `recommendedSize.width`.\\n29: - A relative `above`/`below` `defaultPlacement` requires\\n30:   `recommendedSize.height`.\\n31: - Before choosing a relative placement, inspect the anchor's current Layout\\n32:   parent. `before`/`after` require an existing Row or root anchor;\\n33:   `above`/`below` require an existing Column or root anchor. An anchor inside\\n34:   a root Row cannot use `below` through `insert_plugin_default`. If no\\n35:   supported default fits the requested UI, use the low-level AppUIModel path\\n36:   after reading its Skill instead of declaring an unusable default.\\n37: - A `plugin_slot` placement points at an existing parent child Slot, matches\\n38:   one of that Slot's accepted capabilities, and matches renderer mode.\\n39: - A Plugin with `requiresRenderScope: true` uses a renderer child Slot; a\\n40:   non-renderer Plugin must not target one.\\n41: - A visual Plugin without `authoring` is intentionally `manual-only` and is\\n42:   valid. `authoring` without `defaultPlacement` is discoverable but\\n43:   `limited`, with Add/Restore unavailable.\\n44: - A Plugin with `capabilities: [\\\"headless\\\"]` or `manifest.application.gate` is\\n45:   not a visual placement target and does not need authoring metadata.\\n46: \\n47: Read the readiness diagnostics returned by `validate_creator_changes` through\\n48: the existing `verify:ui` result. A limited warning is not automatically a\\n49: failure: if the Plugin should be Creator-operable, repair its authoring\\n50: contract; if it is intentionally manual-only, do not invent a placement.\\n51: \\n52: \\n53: ## Service dependency and ownership decision\\n54: \\n55: When a Plugin needs another capability, call `inspect_ui_services` instead of\\n56: guessing a Provider from Plugin names or source proximity.\\n57: \\n58: ```text\\n59: Plugin needs capability X\\n60: -> inspect_ui_services\\n61: -> existing Service?\\n62:    -> yes: classify core requirement as inject, enhancement as optionalInject\\n63:    -> no: is a cross-boundary shared Service actually necessary?\\n64:       -> no: keep the behavior private to the Plugin\\n65:       -> yes: resolve the natural Owner, exact Consumers and dependency modes\\n66:               -> prepare_ui_service_contract_change\\n67:               -> ready: create_ui_service_contract using returned planId\\n68:                  -> wire Provider and Consumers\\n69:                  -> validate, then Runtime verify only in static_and_runtime mode\\n70: ```\\n71: \\n72: - `inject` is only for a capability without which the Plugin's core behavior cannot work.\\n73: - `optionalInject` is for an enhancement with a complete fallback when the Service is unavailable.\\n74: - Omit unrelated optional Services; a missing dependency alone is not a reason to add shared behavior.\\n75: - A new Plugin does not declare `provides` merely because another Plugin might use its behavior later.\\n76: - Project Service code is editable for the current task. Optional Service\\n77:   preflight tools check topology, ownership and current hashes without asking\\n78:   the user; apply their returned planId, or use ordinary read/edit/create tools.\\n79: - Supporting Service work does not require another new-Plugin question.\\n80: - To change an existing Service Contract, inspect all `contractPaths`, Providers and Consumers, require one canonical path, read that file, inspect the impact, then use `mutate_ui_service_contract` exact edits and update affected Plugins.\\n81: - Generic project file tools can edit `/services/**`; use the structured tools when their topology or declaration checks are useful.\\n82: - A public Service is justified only across a real boundary: multiple Plugins, another Plugin caller, Application Shell, or a Frontend Tool/Agent adapter. Private state and helpers stay inside the Plugin.\\n83: \\n84: \\n85: ## Creating a Plugin\\n86: \\n87: 1. Classify the Plugin, decide whether it should be Creator-operable, and define its Runtime, Composition, and optional Creator Authoring contracts.\\n88: 2. Use the installed public `UIPluginDefinition`, `UIPluginManifest` and\\n89:    `UIPluginComponentProps` contracts from `@za38.03/agent-ui-plugins/contracts`.\\n90:    For unresolved fields, query those symbols through `inspect_ui_plugin` with\\n91:    that `sharedModule`. Current Bootstrap projects do not contain\\n92:    `/framework/contracts/ui-plugin.ts`. A nearby thin application is sufficient\\n93:    evidence for registration and locale conventions; do not search for a fully\\n94:    copied official implementation. A self-contained local-state Plugin needs\\n95:    only the relevant component, manifest and optional child-Slot contracts.\\n96: 3. For a new visible Plugin, draft its manifest and call `preflight_ui_plugin_placement` with the current AppUIModel hash, capability catalog revision, and intended instance id before writing source. Use its canonical `defaultPlacement` only if it matches the user's location and lifecycle. A rejection requires a same-semantics repair or a user decision; do not switch to application scope, another side, or a conditional Slot as fallback. Preflight does not verify source, Services, or narrow Runtime geometry, and the final mutation checks again. Once eligible, proceed to locale edits and the atomic `create_ui_plugin` call; further source searches need a specific unresolved API.\\n97: 4. Create `/plugins/<plugin-id>/manifest.json` with a unique id, useful description, version, capabilities when applicable, accurate `data.messages`, `data.state`, or `data.events` declarations, and the authoring contract when Add/Restore is intended.\\n98:    `data.messages`, `data.state`, and `data.messageUI` are booleans for AG-UI subscriptions; `data.events` is an array of event names. Local React `useState` is private Plugin state and does not belong in `manifest.data`.\\n99: 5. Create `index.tsx` with a named React component. When an existing project component implements the requested UI, this may be a thin adapter importing it; do not recreate that UI for Plugin self-containment. Accept `UIPluginComponentProps` only when it needs `renderSlot`; read Agent and instance data through Runtime Context hooks in the adapter and narrow unknown state safely.\\n100: 6. Create `definition.ts` that validates the manifest and exports a `UIPluginDefinition`.\\n101:    Import the component with `from \\\"./index\\\"` when it lives in the required `index.tsx`. Do not also create `index.ts` as a barrel: TypeScript resolves `./index` to that file first and can make the component import circular. Do not add a `.tsx` extension to the import; the Host TypeScript configuration does not enable that syntax.\\n102:    Declare every service a built-in hook consumes. `useAgentUILocale` needs `AGENT_UI_LOCALE_SERVICE`; `useAgentUITheme` needs `AGENT_UI_THEME_SERVICE`, each in `inject` or `optionalInject` as appropriate. Theme CSS tokens alone do not need the theme hook.\\n103: 7. Add styles using the generated project's existing styling approach; do not introduce a UI library or dependency without project support.\\n104:    Import a Plugin stylesheet once. For ordinary action buttons, use the\\n105:    project's inspected public Button facade when one exists.\\n106: 8. Default-export the definition so the target-owned generator can include it in the static Registry. Do not spread a template catalog into the production registry.\\n107: 9. Submit `pluginId` and all currently known new Plugin files together in one `create_ui_plugin` call, using `relativePath` values inside that Plugin directory. It requires `manifest.json`, `definition.ts`, and `index.tsx`, is create-only, and transactionally rolls back the whole call on failure. Never use it to replace an existing Plugin directory or file.\\n108: 10. Run `validate_creator_changes`. Fix returned diagnostics with `read_file` plus `edit_file`, then validate the new revision again.\\n109: 11. Add exactly one AppUIPluginNode through `mutate_app_ui_model`. When an\\n110:     eligible relative `defaultPlacement` declares the intended side panel,\\n111:     use `insert_plugin_default` so the Host places it beside the anchor in the\\n112:     public Layout and preserves the Platform drawer track. Do not insert it into\\n113:     the conversation's existing Slot or nest a Row inside its Panel. Use a\\n114:     low-level Layout operation only for a different authorized location after\\n115:     checking its root track and responsive contract. An Application Gate goes\\n116:     at application scope. The transaction updates the generated Registry.\\n117: 12. Because composition changes the Activity revision, run `validate_creator_changes` again for the final revision.\\n118: 13. In `static_and_runtime` mode, call `inspect_runtime_errors`. Fresh current-hash evidence with zero current errors is required before claiming Runtime success. In `static_only` mode, stop after current-revision static validation and do not claim Runtime success.\\n119: \\n120: \\n121: ## Contract boundaries\\n122: \\n123: ### Application Gate contract\\n124: \\n125: Use `manifest.application.gate` when entry to the Workspace must be denied until a condition is ready. Do not model this requirement as a modal, overlay, root/main Slot contribution, dedicated Login Slot, or high-z-index element, and do not change the Layout Tree merely to host it.\\n126: \\n127: - The Gate manifest names one observable Service and may assign a numeric priority. Do not also add an `app-gate` capability; `application.gate` is the single source of truth and is distinct from `headless`.\\n128: - The definition declares the Gate Service in `provides`, and `setup()` synchronously provides it with an initial `checking`, `blocked`, `ready`, or `error` snapshot. Async session recovery begins only after the observable Service is available.\\n129: - A Gate plugin node is `enabled: true` in `applicationPlugins`. A Gate manifest must not declare child Slots.\\n130: - Gate hard dependencies use `inject`. Every Provider in that dependency closure must be an application-scoped `headless` Plugin or another Application Gate. `optionalInject` never expands the startup dependency closure.\\n131: - The Gate component may read its own provided Gate Service with `usePluginService()`. This self-read permission applies to Components only; `setup({ services }).get()` still reads only `inject` and `optionalInject` dependencies.\\n132: - Multiple Gates all must become `ready`. Priority selects which non-ready Gate surface is currently displayed; it does not weaken the all-ready rule.\\n133: - Gate state is frontend Application lifecycle state. Never add AG-UI custom events or modify Agent protocol semantics to control it.\\n134: \\n135: For requests such as “不登录不能进入应用”, “打开应用必须先登录”, “没有 License 不能使用”, “必须先选择组织”, or “初始化完成前不能进入工作台”, inspect existing Gate/Auth assets and Services first, then create or reuse an Application Gate without introducing a Login Slot or Layout overlay.\\n136: \\n137: ### Child Slot contract\\n138: \\n139: When adding, removing, or renaming a child `renderSlot(...)` outlet in a container Plugin, update `manifest.json` `slots.children` in the same task. Each local Slot name maps to a required `description`, `cardinality: \\\"one\\\" | \\\"many\\\"`, and optional `optional` flag. Names must be static string literals; do not create dynamic `renderSlot(slot)` outlets or global Runtime slot ids. Host verification treats the Plugin source and manifest child Slot sets as an exact contract.\\n140: \\n141: For an npm-backed Plugin, the project application may add a new semantic outlet\\n142: without source takeover. Preserve every installed canonical child Slot unchanged,\\n143: declare the additional Slot in the local manifest, expose that combined manifest\\n144: from the local definition, and render the new outlet explicitly in the application.\\n145: Keep forwarding the original props and Slots into the npm component. The Host\\n146: combines only the additional local Slots with the installed canonical contract;\\n147: the npm component does not implicitly render project-added outlets.\\n148: \\n149: For a runtime entity renderer, declare `mode: \\\"renderer\\\"`, `cardinality: \\\"one\\\"`, and `accepts.anyOfCapabilities`, then call `renderScopedSlot(\\\"localName\\\", scope)`. Renderer Slots have no implicit presentation fallback. A fallback must be explicitly supplied by the owning parent; otherwise an empty, disabled, unavailable, inactive, or capability-mismatched occupant renders nothing. Omitted `mode` means ordinary content. The renderer Plugin reads `usePluginRenderScope()` and checks its `kind`; a Plugin that requires scope declares `requiresRenderScope: true` and returns `null` without the expected scope. Prefer changing the renderer Plugin instance in AppUIModel when changing Reasoning, Tool Group, or Tool Fallback presentation. Keep Agent UI grouping, part order, named Tool UI selection, and message lifecycle in the canonical Thread.\\n150: \\n151: - Read Agent data through the installed Runtime public APIs and the project's existing domain/Service Hooks. Inspect their actual exports and signatures; initialized projects do not contain a local `/runtime/context` module. Keep the existing Provider and canonical Conversation Runtime.\\n152: - Read the current instance scope through `usePluginInstance`, `usePluginActions`, and `usePluginEvents`. Never recreate a combined context prop or pass Runtime snapshot fields through component props.\\n153: - Before a Plugin consumes a backend Application Event, inspect the existing `Agent.appEventSchemas` binding and `/agent-contract/agent-events.ts` when present, reuse or add the application-owned payload schema, then declare the same exact name in `manifest.data.events`. Preserve an explicitly supplied Custom Event name exactly; lowercase dot-separated naming is recommended only when the application has not already chosen a name. A manifest declaration consumes an application-owned contract; it does not register one.\\n154: - Prefer the project-local `subscribeAppEvent` helper so the registered payload type is inferred; `usePluginEvents().subscribe` and `setup({ events })` remain the underlying scoped APIs. Never import AG-UI protocol event types into Plugin code, invent a schema inside a Plugin, emit an Application Event from the frontend, or use this channel for persistent state, standard lifecycle, Activity, interrupts, Frontend Tools, or local Plugin communication. If the backend payload contract is unknown, ask for it instead of inventing fields.\\n155: - Use `usePluginActions()` for instance-scoped Agent commands and the installed Runtime's public Host API for application-owned commands. Keep transient UI state out of AppUIModel; use private component state within one Plugin and Services across Plugins. Never create a separate Agent Runtime inside a Plugin.\\n156: - Keep Plugin dependencies in the generated project and follow its current UI stack and versions.\\n157: - Service contracts are stable project-owned capability seams, not concrete\\n158:   Provider Plugins or Runtime Core actions:\\n159:   - `provides` means this Plugin owns and declares one or more capabilities for the current activation lifecycle.\\n160:   - `inject` means this Plugin requires a hard capability dependency before activation.\\n161:   - `optionalInject` means this Plugin can use an enhancement but remains complete and active without it.\\n162:   - `usePluginService()` is runtime capability lookup for component access; `setup({ services })` remains the non-React activation API.\\n163: - Provider rules are strict: if `setup` calls `services.provide`, the Plugin **must** declare the same Service Name in `UIPluginDefinition.provides`.\\n164: - For a hard capability dependency, import its stable Service seam from `/services/*` and declare `inject` on `UIPluginDefinition`; do not import concrete Provider Plugin source.\\n165: - Provider implementations must be exposed only through `setup({ services })` + `services.provide(...)`, and the same Service Name must be declared in `provides`.\\n166: - Optional dependency behavior must declare `optionalInject`, call `usePluginService(...)`, and tolerate `undefined` with a complete fallback.\\n167: - `setup({ services }).get(X)` requires X to appear in `inject` or `optionalInject`. A component `usePluginService(X)` may also read a Service declared by its own definition in `provides`; Application-owned lookup remains unrestricted.\\n168: - `provides`, `inject`, and `optionalInject` are pairwise disjoint.\\n169: - When multiple Plugins share a capability, reuse an existing seam name/type from `/services/*` and never invent a synonym service contract.\\n170: - Prefer `UIPluginObservableService` only when other Plugins need sustained observation of service-owned state.\\n171: - `UIPluginObservableService` requires `getSnapshot()` + `subscribe()`; otherwise prefer a structural interface with explicit methods.\\n172: - Structural interface service examples are acceptable, and `EventEmitter`-style ad-hoc emitters should remain project-local, not runtime API additions.\\n173: - Do not place capability implementations into Plugin actions or Agent Runtime actions.\\n174: - A Frontend Tool is an Agent-facing adapter for a selected capability operation; it is not a Plugin capability and is not registered by a Plugin.\\n175: - When the product explicitly asks the Agent to invoke frontend behavior, first reuse an existing stable Service seam, have the Provider Plugin declare `provides`, and expose the selected operation from `/agent-contract/agent-tools.ts`. Implement necessary project Service code with ordinary project tools.\\n176: - Frontend Tool names use `lower_snake_case`, inputs use `z.strictObject(...)`, descriptions explain when to call the Tool plus what it does and does not do, and results stay short, structured, and serializable.\\n177: - Frontend Tool handlers call `services.get(...)` and must tolerate a capability disappearing before execution. Never bind a Tool to a React component, ref, DOM query, Plugin instance, or concrete Provider implementation.\\n178: - Do not automatically expose every Service method. A Service may have zero, one, or many explicitly authorized Frontend Tools.\\n179: - Never generate `context.tools.register(...)`, `services.registerTool(...)`, `plugin.registerTool(...)`, or another Plugin self-registration API. Frontend Tool exposure is an Application permission boundary.\\n180: - Provider implementation lifetime is the Plugin activation lifetime; consumers should always read through runtime services instead of direct imports.\\n181: - Do not couple a generated Plugin to Creator packages or Creator UI dependencies.\\n182: - Do not modify `/runtime` or `/framework` for Plugin-specific behavior.\\n183: - Do not rewrite unrelated registration entries.\\n184: - Hiding, removing an instance, and replacing a feature all preserve Plugin source. Do not delete a Plugin directory with generic file tools. Permanent source deletion may only use the dedicated gated domain tool after exact authorization and reference checks; if that tool is unavailable, report the gate instead of approximating it.\\n185: \\n186: \\n187: ## Frontend Tool capability consumers\\n188: \\n189: A Plugin capability consumed by an application-owned Frontend Tool is a valid\\n190: cross-boundary reason for a public Service seam: Plugin provides Service,\\n191: Frontend Tool consumes Service. Preserve Service ownership and public contracts;\\n192: Service existence does not grant Agent exposure permission.\\n193: \\n194: Plugin Component effects manage UI/component lifecycle only. They must never\\n195: simulate Frontend Tool execution by opening a dialog, navigating or mutating a\\n196: capability when a Tool renderer mounts. Follow the `ag-ui-frontend` skill's\\n197: Frontend Tool execution and replay contract. For integration details, read the\\n198: packaged [Frontend Tool lifecycle reference](../../ag-ui-frontend/references/frontend-tool-lifecycle.md).\\n199: \\n200: \\n201: \\n202: ## Delivery obligations\\n203: \\n204: For new-Plugin gap discovery, `inspect_ui_capabilities({})` returns two current\\n205: Operator results: plugins from list_ui_plugins and services from inspect_ui_services.\\n206: It has no detail or cursor parameters and does not include formal Source Items\\n207: or a project component index. Use inspect_agent_ui_sources separately for formal\\n208: resources and targeted file tools for project components. Do not infer absence\\n209: from this combined Plugin/Service result alone. Filenames never prove component\\n210: behavior. Inspect the selected implementation and reuse it whenever it fits.\\n211: \\n212: Existing project Plugin behavior is edited directly. Ask `prepare_ui_plugin_development` only when creating a new Plugin identity, then implement its necessary supporting files and parent edits. File lists are implementation notes, not permissions. Formal Source Item lookup is needed only for a relevant unknown resource.\\n213: \\n214: Before creating a new Plugin, `prepare_ui_plugin_development` requires only\\n215: `targetPluginId` and a short `desiredOutcome`. Ask whether to develop it; do not\\n216: settle implementation dependencies or test design merely to ask this question.\\n217: Optional `deliveryContract` details may be supplied after confirmation without\\n218: asking again: capability, renderingCategory, placement, lifecycle, dependencies,\\n219: verificationMethod, interactions and optional geometry. They are verification\\n220: inputs, never file permissions.\\n221: \\n222: Creation, registration, enabled composition, static validation, Runtime observation,\\n223: and behavioral validation are separate facts. The Host completion gate derives\\n224: delivery from those facts at the current revision. `static_only` cannot establish\\n225: Runtime or interaction success. Missing placement or verification leaves a blocked\\n226: receipt naming saved changes and the last successful stage.\\n227: \\n228: For declared interactions, the Host tool `verify_ui_plugin_behavior` runs the\\n229: project's installed Playwright configuration. Test titles must exactly match\\n230: `[delivery:<pluginId>] <interaction>`; use `runtime` when the browser-test method has\\n231: no interaction list. Every required test must pass without skips or flaky retries.\\n232: Reuse existing test infrastructure and respect the writable project boundary;\\n233: if tests/configuration cannot be supplied within that boundary, report the blocker.\\n234: Do not replace an absent browser test with a model-written PASS claim. Composer\\n235: adapters require send, stop, attachment add/remove/attachment-only send, and draft\\n236: parity for the capabilities already enabled in the target project.\\n237: \\n238: \\n239: ## Localization ownership\\n240: \\n241: Vendor-owned code must remain locale-agnostic and unmodified; all product\\n242: localization is owned by AgentUICreator composition, adapters, plugins, and Host\\n243: UI. New user-visible copy must use the existing `useAgentUILocale` namespace and\\n244: complete `en-US` / `zh-CN` dictionaries. Do not create per-Plugin i18n hooks or\\n245: language conditionals. Preserve protocol IDs, enums, schema and persisted fields;\\n246: locale is a view concern. Prefer public props/composition, then owned adapters.\\n247: If upstream lacks a seam, record a localization gap instead of modifying vendor.\\n248: Include `pnpm check:i18n` where available and check new copy during upgrades.\\n249: \\n250: \\n251: ## Non-React Hosts\\n252: \\n253: For Vue, HTML and legacy Hosts, discover/install the `web-component-bridge` compatibility resource through the existing resource protocol. React remains the canonical Plugin implementation. Never generate Vue Plugins, Agent UI Vue components or a second AG-UI client. Edit the generated project's AppUIModel and React Plugins, then rebuild its compatibility bundle with `vite build --config <sourceRoot>/integrations/web-component-bridge/vite.config.ts`. The consumer loads the compiled bundle and uses `<agent-ui>`; it does not configure React, JSX, Tailwind or TSX. The build configuration belongs to the Agent UI producer project, not the Vue consumer.\\n254: \\n255: \\n256: For Host integration requests, consume `plan_agent_ui_integration` before editing.\\n257: A how-to guide presents the Host's canonical `edits.after` without writes. Resolve\\n258: an explicit target and compiled module; for automatic integration present the\\n259: same recipe, obtain authorization, then use `apply_agent_ui_integration`. Check\\n260: manual or automatic changes with `verify_agent_ui_integration` and the original\\n261: recipe. Never generate Vue-specific snippets, infer Host versions/entries in the\\n262: prompt, or install the React producer build closure into the Vue consumer. React\\n263: uses its canonical Source Item path; Nuxt is unsupported. Read the packaged\\n264: [Host integration recipes](../../task-guidance/references/host-integration.md)\\n265: and use the current plan result for exact project-specific edits.\\n266: \",\"hash\":\"5bec5caae6352380c2e2b0ac5afe9c767896123b48b55902b20be4a4d73e5ea6\",\"totalLines\":266,\"nextOffset\":null}}","role":"tool"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_START","toolCallId":"call_8612e65148bd4d0d8ef15cc8","toolCallName":"read_file"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_ARGS","toolCallId":"call_8612e65148bd4d0d8ef15cc8","delta":"{\"file_path\":\"/skills/ag-ui-frontend/SKILL.md\",\"limit\":400,\"offset\":0}"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_END","toolCallId":"call_8612e65148bd4d0d8ef15cc8"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_RESULT","toolCallId":"call_8612e65148bd4d0d8ef15cc8","messageId":"a4747b93-b3d5-4367-9cd6-5b519e32c326","content":"{\"ok\":true,\"result\":{\"path\":\"/skills/ag-ui-frontend/SKILL.md\",\"content\":\"1: ---\\n2: name: ag-ui-frontend\\n3: description: Use for frontend consumption of AG-UI messages, shared state, run status, executions, interrupts, and controlled Application Events, Frontend Tools, Agent-controlled frontend capabilities, and browser/client tool execution through project Runtime hooks.\\n4: compatibility: One AG-UI Agent Runtime per generated frontend; Phase 8 permits Plugin source writes while Runtime remains read-only.\\n5: allowed-tools: enable_creator_tools read_file ls glob grep edit_file\\n6: ---\\n7: \\n8: # AG-UI Frontend\\n9: \\n10: Maintain this data flow:\\n11: \\n12: ```text\\n13: AG-UI / Mock transport -> Agent Runtime -> Runtime Context Hooks -> UI Plugin -> project UI stack\\n14: ```\\n15: \\n16: ## Current project contract\\n17: \\n18: - `useAgentMessages()` returns normalized Agent messages, including user, assistant, reasoning, activity, and tool-related messages represented by Runtime Core.\\n19: - `useAgentState<TState>()` returns shared Agent state and must be narrowed safely before property access.\\n20: - `useAgentRun().status` is `idle`, `running`, `awaiting-input`, or `error`; a run error carries the user-visible failure detail.\\n21: - `useAgentExecutions()` exposes each Tool, Reasoning, Step, and Subagent lifecycle directly. Never replace local execution status with a generic Run loading flag.\\n22: - `useAgentInterrupts()` returns only current pending interrupts.\\n23: - `usePluginActions()` exposes instance-scoped Agent commands; `useAgentRuntimeActions()` exposes only Agent Runtime commands. Runtime-only UI state belongs in a named service or runtime store, not in AppUIModel composition.\\n24: - `usePluginEvents()` is the inbound, live-only Application Event channel. A Plugin may subscribe only to names declared in its manifest and registered by `/agent-contract/agent-events.ts`.\\n25: - `usePluginService()` remains the component API for named Plugin capabilities.\\n26: - `/agent-contract/agent-tools.ts` owns the explicit set of frontend capability operations advertised to the Agent.\\n27: \\n28: ## Boundaries\\n29: \\n30: - Do not instantiate an AG-UI client or manage a second Agent Runtime inside a Plugin.\\n31: - Do not invent a separate public message or state protocol when the Plugin Contract already exposes AG-UI data.\\n32: - Event, snapshot, delta, streaming, tool-call, and error normalization belongs in the frontend Runtime layer; Plugins render the normalized hook values.\\n33: - Never expose or import AG-UI `CUSTOM`, `CustomEvent`, `BaseEvent`, `RawEvent`, `rawEvent`, or `@ag-ui/core` from Plugin code. For a backend-originated, application-specific, transient occurrence with no standard AG-UI semantic, define its payload schema in `/agent-contract/agent-events.ts` before declaring it in `manifest.data.events`. Preserve explicitly supplied event names exactly; recommend lowercase dot-separated naming only when the application has not chosen a name, and never guess an unknown backend payload shape.\\n34: - Frontend Tools use standard AG-UI `RunAgentInput.tools`, `TOOL_CALL_*`, `ToolMessage`, and a continuation Run. Never encode a frontend Tool call as `CUSTOM`, a new UserMessage, or a Plugin-specific protocol.\\n35: - The Agent calls a capability adapter, never React or a Plugin. Reuse or define a stable Service seam, let a Provider Plugin declare and provide that Service, then expose only the product-authorized operations in `/agent-contract/agent-tools.ts`.\\n36: - Tool names use `lower_snake_case`; schemas use `z.strictObject(...)` as the single validation and JSON Schema source; handlers resolve Services at execution time and return short serializable results.\\n37: - Do not infer that every Service method should be exposed. Capability ownership and Agent exposure permission are separate decisions.\\n38: - Plugins must never self-register Frontend Tools or import AG-UI Tool types. They only provide capabilities through the existing Service lifecycle.\\n39: - Do not use Application Events for persistent state, activity progress, standard lifecycle, local Plugin-to-Plugin communication, or frontend-to-backend commands. Application Events have no replay, persistence, or history.\\n40: - A request for different layout or placement is not an AG-UI Runtime change.\\n41: - During Phase 8, implement AG-UI presentation inside `/plugins/` while keeping Runtime and Framework source read-only.\\n42: \\n43: For interaction semantics, reason from product needs and AG-UI first. Treat external Agent UI projects as references, not runtime dependencies or public contracts.\\n44: \\n45: ## History, attachments, speech and feedback services\\n46: \\n47: These capabilities have built-in defaults and project-owned service wiring.\\n48: Business URLs/auth belong in implementation code, not Agent props. Before connecting a business history, upload, transcription or feedback\\n49: service, read [service configuration](references/service-configuration.md).\\n50: Users supply endpoint/authentication/payload details. Creator edits wiring and uses the confirmed private implementation change tools\\n51: for services/ HTTP integration. Explain operation and shared impact before\\n52: confirmation; public Service contract changes use their separate tools. Report\\n53: applied changes and verification accurately. Preserve explicit custom adapters and Plugin layout.\\n54: \\n55: ## Frontend Tool execution and replay contract\\n56: \\n57: For capability or renderer integration details, read the packaged\\n58: [Frontend Tool lifecycle reference](references/frontend-tool-lifecycle.md).\\n59: \\n60: 1. Product side effects belong only in Tool `execute`: open dialog, navigate,\\n61:    select item, change editor file, modify application state, write storage, copy\\n62:    clipboard, download, call mutation API, submit form, or trigger a host action.\\n63: 2. A Tool renderer is a replayable projection. Assume history load, thread/branch\\n64:    switch, remount, React StrictMode and virtualization can mount it again.\\n65:    `render / mount useEffect != tool invocation`.\\n66: 3. Never implement Agent behavior with\\n67:    `useEffect(() => capability.doSomething(), [])`. Instead use\\n68:    `execute -> capability.doSomething()` and `render -> args/result/status`.\\n69:    UI measurement, ResizeObserver, subscription cleanup, focus and animation\\n70:    effects remain legitimate.\\n71: 4. Results must carry a historical receipt sufficient to render args/result/status\\n72:    without checking today's application state. An opened dialog result can contain\\n73:    `{ opened: true, title: \\\"Settings\\\" }`; history does not require it to stay open.\\n74: 5. History restoration must never execute Frontend Tools. An execute on history\\n75:    load is a Runtime bug; do not patch it with renderer `isHistory` workarounds.\\n76: 6. Keep `Plugin -> Service`, `Application agent-tools -> allowed operation`,\\n77:    `Runtime adapter -> Agent UI native frontend execute`. Plugins never\\n78:    register Tools. Preserve root-only execution scope until subagent attribution\\n79:    has its own contract. Do not add another TOOL_CALL executor or CUSTOM protocol.\\n80: \\n81: ## Official integrations\\n82: \\n83: When Agent UI provides a specialized integration:\\n84: \\n85: 1. Inspect the official integration at the project's pinned upstream revision first.\\n86: 2. Reuse its public semantic contracts where possible.\\n87: 3. Do not bypass AgentUICreator application permission.\\n88: 4. Model reusable adaptation as an Integration, not as a Demo Plugin.\\n89: \\n90: React Hook Form: reuse `@za38.03/agent-ui-plugins/form-tools.formTools` through\\n91: `integration/react-hook-form`. Use ordinary RHF `useForm()` plus an application\\n92: Service and `createReactHookFormFrontendTools({ serviceName, fields, expose })`.\\n93: `expose` is mandatory and has no default. Do not call `useAssistantForm()` or\\n94: `aui.modelContext.register(...)`; combining those with the factory creates duplicate\\n95: registration and bypasses application permission.\\n96: \\n97: Integration installation != automatic Agent permission. Only application\\n98: frontend-tool modules included in the generated allowlist expose operations.\\n99: Integrations can be pluginless and tool-less. Demo consumers own UI, field names,\\n100: Service lifecycle and replayable receipts; the Integration owns reusable contracts.\\n101: \\n102: ## Agent UI Generative UI\\n103: \\n104: `@za38.03/agent-ui-plugins/generative-ui` is the canonical component vocabulary and\\n105: renderer. Prefer official vocabulary before creating custom components. Inspect\\n106: the project's installed version: do not infer capabilities from unreleased\\n107: source or from the presence of related CSS selectors.\\n108: \\n109: - Install optional `integration/generative-ui` for the shared factory, official\\n110:   styled vocabulary, Action Registry and public component export. These files\\n111:   are managed together as one optional Integration, not a default Foundation.\\n112: - `createAgentUIGenerativeUI` exposes `present()` and `promptUser()` capabilities.\\n113:   Capability availability does not grant Agent permission. Do not automatically\\n114:   mount or advertise `present` / `prompt_user`; explicit Tool authorization requires a separate product decision.\\n115: - Use the pinned package types and official Form `$input` collection; do not infer availability from CSS selectors.\\n116: - Keep optional source provenance and official styles under Source Registry;\\n117:   regenerate from the pinned package-owned upstream source. CSS permits only\\n118:   mechanical `.agent-ui-conversation` selector scoping, with no visual redesign.\\n119: \\n120: ## Localization ownership\\n121: \\n122: Vendor-owned code must remain locale-agnostic and unmodified; all product\\n123: localization is owned by AgentUICreator composition, adapters, plugins, and Host\\n124: UI. New user-visible copy must use the existing `useAgentUILocale` namespace and\\n125: complete `en-US` / `zh-CN` dictionaries. Do not create per-Plugin i18n hooks or\\n126: language conditionals. Preserve protocol IDs, enums, schema and persisted fields;\\n127: locale is a view concern. Prefer public props/composition, then owned adapters.\\n128: If upstream lacks a seam, record a localization gap instead of modifying vendor.\\n129: Include `pnpm check:i18n` where available and check new copy during upgrades.\\n130: \",\"hash\":\"377c0469041a453cec1e9c7389fb58927779bacfa14f4c59c69dc22c8bea077f\",\"totalLines\":130,\"nextOffset\":null}}","role":"tool"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_START","toolCallId":"call_7e02a2417e88401ebab41423","toolCallName":"read_creator_context"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_ARGS","toolCallId":"call_7e02a2417e88401ebab41423","delta":"{\"artifactId\":\"ctx-6b3588e4a11aa63f2606201507c097352f076cea325b422a26add181b1cbc901\",\"path\":[\"result\",\"content\"],\"offset\":0,\"limit\":8000}"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_END","toolCallId":"call_7e02a2417e88401ebab41423"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_RESULT","toolCallId":"call_7e02a2417e88401ebab41423","messageId":"954f5875-01d7-4bdf-9760-5bc739eff5e8","content":"{\"ok\":true,\"result\":{\"artifactId\":\"ctx-6b3588e4a11aa63f2606201507c097352f076cea325b422a26add181b1cbc901\",\"historical\":true,\"grantsFreshness\":false,\"originalTool\":\"read_file\",\"path\":[\"result\",\"content\"],\"kind\":\"string\",\"keys\":[],\"keyCount\":0,\"content\":\"1: # Task-specific Plugin authoring contracts\\n2: \\n3: Consult only sections relevant to the actual change. Creating a new Plugin,\\n4: changing a Service or binding Agent data has obligations beyond an existing\\n5: owned presentation edit. This reference grants no authority.\\n6: \\n7: ## Creator Authoring Contract\\n8: \\n9: Runtime-compatible, Composition-compatible, and Creator-operable are separate\\n10: decisions:\\n11: \\n12: - Runtime-compatible means the Plugin can be activated by the UI Runtime.\\n13: - Composition-compatible means its Layout or child Slot contract is valid.\\n14: - Creator-operable means the Host can discover, add, and restore it\\n15:   deterministically.\\n16: \\n17: The existing `manifest.authoring` fields are the Creator Authoring Contract.\\n18: Do not add `creatorReady`, a readiness score, or Plugin-specific Creator logic.\\n19: The Host derives readiness from the manifest, capabilities, and child Slot\\n20: contracts; it does not inspect React source to guess placement.\\n21: \\n22: Before writing source, classify the Plugin and decide whether users should be\\n23: able to ask Creator to add or restore it:\\n24: \\n25: - A visual Plugin intended for natural-language Add/Restore declares semantic\\n26:   `authoring.intents` and a deterministic `defaultPlacement`.\\n27: - A relative `before`/`after` `defaultPlacement` requires\\n28:   `recommendedSize.width`.\\n29: - A relative `above`/`below` `defaultPlacement` requires\\n30:   `recommendedSize.height`.\\n31: - Before choosing a relative placement, inspect the anchor's current Layout\\n32:   parent. `before`/`after` require an existing Row or root anchor;\\n33:   `above`/`below` require an existing Column or root anchor. An anchor inside\\n34:   a root Row cannot use `below` through `insert_plugin_default`. If no\\n35:   supported default fits the requested UI, use the low-level AppUIModel path\\n36:   after reading its Skill instead of declaring an unusable default.\\n37: - A `plugin_slot` placement points at an existing parent child Slot, matches\\n38:   one of that Slot's accepted capabilities, and matches renderer mode.\\n39: - A Plugin with `requiresRenderScope: true` uses a renderer child Slot; a\\n40:   non-renderer Plugin must not target one.\\n41: - A visual Plugin without `authoring` is intentionally `manual-only` and is\\n42:   valid. `authoring` without `defaultPlacement` is discoverable but\\n43:   `limited`, with Add/Restore unavailable.\\n44: - A Plugin with `capabilities: [\\\"headless\\\"]` or `manifest.application.gate` is\\n45:   not a visual placement target and does not need authoring metadata.\\n46: \\n47: Read the readiness diagnostics returned by `validate_creator_changes` through\\n48: the existing `verify:ui` result. A limited warning is not automatically a\\n49: failure: if the Plugin should be Creator-operable, repair its authoring\\n50: contract; if it is intentionally manual-only, do not invent a placement.\\n51: \\n52: \\n53: ## Service dependency and ownership decision\\n54: \\n55: When a Plugin needs another capability, call `inspect_ui_services` instead of\\n56: guessing a Provider from Plugin names or source proximity.\\n57: \\n58: ```text\\n59: Plugin needs capability X\\n60: -> inspect_ui_services\\n61: -> existing Service?\\n62:    -> yes: classify core requirement as inject, enhancement as optionalInject\\n63:    -> no: is a cross-boundary shared Service actually necessary?\\n64:       -> no: keep the behavior private to the Plugin\\n65:       -> yes: resolve the natural Owner, exact Consumers and dependency modes\\n66:               -> prepare_ui_service_contract_change\\n67:               -> ready: create_ui_service_contract using returned planId\\n68:                  -> wire Provider and Consumers\\n69:                  -> validate, then Runtime verify only in static_and_runtime mode\\n70: ```\\n71: \\n72: - `inject` is only for a capability without which the Plugin's core behavior cannot work.\\n73: - `optionalInject` is for an enhancement with a complete fallback when the Service is unavailable.\\n74: - Omit unrelated optional Services; a missing dependency alone is not a reason to add shared behavior.\\n75: - A new Plugin does not declare `provides` merely because another Plugin might use its behavior later.\\n76: - Project Service code is editable for the current task. Optional Service\\n77:   preflight tools check topology, ownership and current hashes without asking\\n78:   the user; apply their returned planId, or use ordinary read/edit/create tools.\\n79: - Supporting Service work does not require another new-Plugin question.\\n80: - To change an existing Service Contract, inspect all `contractPaths`, Providers and Consumers, require one canonical path, read that file, inspect the impact, then use `mutate_ui_service_contract` exact edits and update affected Plugins.\\n81: - Generic project file tools can edit `/services/**`; use the structured tools when their topology or declaration checks are useful.\\n82: - A public Service is justified only across a real boundary: multiple Plugins, another Plugin caller, Application Shell, or a Frontend Tool/Agent adapter. Private state and helpers stay inside the Plugin.\\n83: \\n84: \\n85: ## Creating a Plugin\\n86: \\n87: 1. Classify the Plugin, decide whether it should be Creator-operable, and define its Runtime, Composition, and optional Creator Authoring contracts.\\n88: 2. Use the installed public `UIPluginDefinition`, `UIPluginManifest` and\\n89:    `UIPluginComponentProps` contracts from `@za38.03/agent-ui-plugins/contracts`.\\n90:    For unresolved fields, query those symbols through `inspect_ui_plugin` with\\n91:    that `sharedModule`. Current Bootstrap projects do not contain\\n92:    `/framework/contracts/ui-plugin.ts`. A nearby thin application is sufficient\\n93:    evidence for registration and locale conventions; do not search for a fully\\n94:    copied official implementation. A self-contained local-state Plugin needs\\n95:    only the relevant component, manifest and optional child-Slot contracts.\\n96: 3. For a new visible Plugin, draft its manifest and call `preflight_ui_plugin_placement` with the current AppUIModel hash, capability catalog revision, and intended instance id before writing source. Use its canonical `defaultPlacement` only if it matches the user's location and lifecycle. A rejection requires a same-semantics repair or a user decision; do not switch to application scope, another side, or a conditional Slot as fallback. Preflight does not verify source, Services, or narrow Runtime geometry, and the final mutation checks again. Once eligible, proceed to locale edits and the atomic `create_ui_plugin` call; further source searches need a specific unresolved API.\\n97: 4. Create `/plugins/<plugin-id>/manifest.json` with a unique id, useful description, version, capabilities when applicable, accurate `data.messages`, `data.state`, or `data.events` declarations, and the authoring contract when Add/Restore is intended.\\n98:    `data.messages`, `data.state`, and `data.messageUI` are booleans for AG-UI subscriptions; `data.events` is an array of event names. Local React `useState` is private Plugin state and does not belong in `manifest.data`.\\n99: 5. Create `index.tsx` with a named React component. When an existing project component implements the requested UI, this may be a thin adapter importing it; do not recreate that UI for Plugin self-containment. Accept `UIPluginComponentProps` only when it needs `renderSlot`; read Agent and instance data through Runtime Context hooks in the adapter and narrow unknown state safely.\\n100: 6. Create `definition.ts` that validates the manifest and exports a `UIPluginDefinition`.\\n101:    Import the component with `from \\\"./index\\\"` when it lives in the required `index.tsx`. Do not also create `index.ts` as a barrel: TypeScript resolves `./index` to that file first and can make the component import circular. Do not add a `.tsx` extension to the import; the Host TypeScript configuration does not enable that syntax.\\n102:    Declare every service a built-in hook consumes. `useAgentUILocale` needs `AGENT_UI_LOCALE\",\"offset\":0,\"nextOffset\":8000,\"totalCharacters\":26123}}","role":"tool"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_START","toolCallId":"call_b2e69fb2336d4fe6b321b086","toolCallName":"read_creator_context"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_ARGS","toolCallId":"call_b2e69fb2336d4fe6b321b086","delta":"{\"artifactId\":\"ctx-6b3588e4a11aa63f2606201507c097352f076cea325b422a26add181b1cbc901\",\"limit\":8000,\"offset\":8000,\"path\":[\"result\",\"content\"]}"}

